  "articleSummary": "**Summary:**  \nThe article argues that hiring an AI engineer should focus on evaluating the candidate’s judgment and ownership of real‑world AI work, not just impressive demos or résumés. It proposes a concrete hiring process: define the concrete outcome you need (e.g., a pilot that reduces handling time without increasing errors), then test candidates with a hands‑on exercise that reveals how they design, measure, and troubleshoot a shipped AI system. The piece breaks down the kinds of AI roles (research, application engineering, infrastructure, evaluation, product) and the specific evidence to look for in each, and supplies five interview questions that probe failure handling, evaluation rigor, and permission boundaries. The tone is a practical tutorial aimed at product managers, founders, and engineering leaders who must assess AI talent without deep technical expertise. Recurring metaphors include “opening the hood” and “reading the trace” to distinguish superficial demos from observable, accountable work.",
